# Files and directories that are NOT part of the released plugin.
#
# What ships is the runtime PHP, the compiled block assets in build/, the
# compiled stylesheets and scripts in assets/build/, the vendored Prism library
# in assets/lib/prism/, templates/, the readmes, the changelog and the licence
# files.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW THIS FILE IS ACTUALLY READ — it is NOT a .gitignore
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Both 10up actions we use (action-wordpress-plugin-build-zip and
# action-wordpress-plugin-deploy) feed this file straight to rsync:
#
#   rsync -rc --exclude-from=.distignore "$GITHUB_WORKSPACE/" trunk/ \
#         --delete --delete-excluded
#
# So the rules here are **rsync filter patterns**, with these consequences:
#
#   * The source is the working tree ON DISK, not `git archive`. Anything
#     present in the checkout is copied unless excluded here — git's opinion of
#     it is irrelevant. This is precisely why build/ ships: it is .gitignored,
#     it is generated by `npm run build` during the release job, and because
#     this file exists (and does not list it) rsync picks it up.
#
#     If this file were ever deleted, both actions fall back to a
#     `git archive` + .gitattributes export, which CANNOT see build/ — and the
#     plugin would ship with no block. Do not delete .distignore.
#
#   * There is NO negation. rsync's --exclude-from has no `!` form and the
#     10up/WP-CLI convention does not add one. Every rule below only ever
#     removes things; nothing here can add something back.
#
#   * A pattern with no slash matches that BASENAME at any depth (a trailing
#     slash does not count as a slash for this purpose). `vendor/` removes a
#     `vendor` directory wherever it appears, not just at the root — and `src/`
#     removes assets/src/ as well as the root src/, which is intended and is
#     spelled out again lower down. Checked: apart from that, nothing under
#     assets/, classes/, templates/, build/ or assets/build/ collides with any
#     basename listed here.
#
#   * A pattern containing a slash is matched against the tail of the path, so
#     `assets/src/` means "any path ending .../assets/src/".
#
#   * A trailing slash restricts the rule to directories.
#
#   * `*` is a glob within one path segment; it does not cross `/`.
#
#   * Blank lines and lines beginning with `#` are ignored by rsync.
#

# Version control and forge config.
# `.git*` covers .git, .gitignore, .gitattributes, .gitmodules and .github;
# the explicit entries below are kept for readability, not because they add
# coverage.
.git*
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.gitmodules
.github/

# WordPress.org listing assets (banners, icons, screenshots). These belong in
# SVN's /assets, never in the plugin itself — the deploy action reads them
# straight from the checkout, so excluding them here is correct.
.wordpress-org/

# Local tooling, editor and agent scratch space. None of it is even committed,
# but a developer's working copy has it and rsync copies from disk.
#
# `.specs/` holds the PRD, the implementation plan and the session log. It is a
# separate line from `.scratchpad/` and not a rename of it: the documents moved
# out of the scratch directory and, in moving, left the exclusion that had been
# covering them. Being in `.gitignore` is no help here — rsync reads the disk.
.specs/
.scratchpad/
.claude/
.ai/
.agents/
CLAUDE.md
Makefile
.editorconfig
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Dev dependencies. Composer is dev-only (no runtime PHP dependencies) and the
# block is bundled by wp-scripts, so neither tree belongs in the zip.
node_modules/
vendor/

# Sources that are compiled before release.
#   src/        -> build/          (wp-scripts / TypeScript)
#   assets/src/ -> assets/build/   (sass for the scss, terser for the js)
#
# Both source trees are excluded and both output trees SHIP. Neither output tree
# is committed — .gitignore covers them — so both exist only because the release
# job runs `npm run build` before the packaging action rsyncs the workspace. That
# is the same arrangement build/ has always had, and the same reason this file
# must not be deleted (see the header).
#
# `src/` has no internal slash, so by the basename rule above it already removes
# assets/src/ as well. It is listed a second time with its full path because a
# rule that only works by accident of another rule's matching semantics is a rule
# waiting to be broken; either line alone is sufficient.
#
# Nothing here excludes `build`, at the root or under assets/. Checked against
# every pattern in this file: none of them matches the basename `build`.
src/
assets/src/

# Build-tool and test-runner config.
package.json
package-lock.json
composer.json
composer.lock
phpcs.xml
phpcs.xml.dist
phpunit.xml
phpunit.xml.dist
tsconfig.json
# Ambient TypeScript declarations consumed only by `tsc --noEmit`. Checked:
# nothing under assets/, classes/, templates/ or build/ is named `types`.
types/
.nvmrc
.distignore

# Tests and tooling caches.
tests/
.phpunit.cache
.phpunit.result.cache
.phpcs.cache

# Scratch directories created inside the workspace by the release workflow.
# In the current workflow neither can reach the archive (the build-zip action
# runs its rsync before `zipfile/` exists, and the deploy job uses BUILD_DIR,
# which bypasses this file entirely). They are listed so that stays true if
# anyone later drops BUILD_DIR.
zipfile/
dist/
